WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.300
Marvel's Runaways is being run off the air.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:08.260
On Monday, November 18, Hulu announced that
Runaways will end after its fast-approaching

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:13.200
third season, set to hit the Disney-owned
streaming platform on December 13.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.840
Deadline confirmed the news, further noting
that fans will get closure with Runaways season

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.710
3, which is said to bring the narrative to
a, quote, "natural close" and neatly wrap

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:27.810
up the story that follows six teen heroes
whose parents belong to a coalition of super-villains.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.980
So, what really happened to push Marvel TV
and Hulu to axe the show?

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.560
Well, you can blame the numbers for that,
it's always the numbers.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:41.260
Sure, fans adored the show, and critics were
kind to it, but Runaways was actually performing

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.320
worse than its fellow Marvel superhero series.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.380
You know, the ones that were all canceled
at Netflix between late 2018 and early 2019.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.620
In a March 2019 data report, Business Insider
provided analytics from Jumpshot that indicated

00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:01.110
roughly 640,000 people watched the first season
of Runaways in its first week on Hulu in November

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:07.979
2017, with 825,000 people tuning into the
second season during its first week in December

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:08.840
2018.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.660
That may sound like a lot of people, but it
isn't.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:16.660
Even the bumped-up viewership number for Marvel's
Runaways season 2 was only about half the

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.289
amount of people who watched the least-viewed
Marvel show on Netflix: the highly criticized

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:26.640
Iron Fist, which pulled in 1.6 million viewers
in the first week after dropping its second

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.180
season.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.940
Unsurprisingly, Runaways couldn't hold a candle
to the most-watched Netflix-Marvel show at

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.880
the time, Daredevil, with 5.8 million week-one
viewers for season 2.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:40.840
"No!"

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.620
"That good, huh?"

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.180
"You sunk the 8-ball."

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.760
"Well…something went in."

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.299
Business Insider noted that despite rising
interest from season 1 to season 2 of Marvel's

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.580
Runaways, neither installment ever reached
the million-viewer mark in the first week.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.200
This could be, as the outlet stated, down
to declining viewership for all of Marvel's

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.160
streaming series.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.760
Viewership trends that were "headed in the
wrong direction" for the superhero series

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:10.110
on Netflix, followed by the rapid-fire cancelations
of every single Marvel-Netflix show, may have

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.540
pushed more casual viewers away from Runaways,
possibly because they feared the show would

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.460
soon suffer the same fate as Daredevil and
the like.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.560
Additionally, it isn't as though Runaways
wasn't interesting or exciting.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.780
Impressive reviews and a loyal fanbase prove
the opposite, but the "diverging strategies"

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.189
that Netflix and Hulu implemented with their
respective Marvel properties may have been

00:02:31.189 --> 00:02:35.820
the downfall of the combined collection of
superhero shows, Marvel's Runaways included.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.400
Despite the show's low ratings, Hulu still
renewed Runaways for a third season, though

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.430
it seems the streamer may have ordered those
final 10 episodes with the knowledge that

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.740
they'd be the last ones people would ever
watch.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:51.580
On that note, it's been clear for quite some
time now that Marvel Television is experiencing

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.580
major shake-ups.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.160
The creation of Disney's streaming platform
Disney+ meant that the Mouse House, which

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:02.350
owns Marvel Studios and its Marvel Television
division, would be creating new original series

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:07.329
based on its gallery of superheroes, all to
be produced by Marvel Studios, led by Marvel

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:12.000
Entertainment chief creative officer Kevin
Feige, rather than Marvel Television, headed

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.460
up by executive vice president Jeph Loeb.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.800
In an apparent effort to consolidate its various
properties and bring them to one place, Marvel

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.569
previously cleared house at Netflix by pulling
the plug on Daredevil, Iron Fist, Luke Cage,

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:27.300
Jessica Jones, and The Punisher.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.790
With those shows done at Netflix, fans would
surely have to turn elsewhere to get their

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.640
superhero series fix, and where better to
look than the massive streamer that will be

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.260
home to a ton of Marvel shows?

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.740
Could Marvel Studios executives have chatted
with the TV division and the folks at Hulu

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:46.500
about bringing Runaways to an end to further
clear the path for Disney+'s Marvel universe

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.120
to thrive?

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.060
It's not out of the realm of reality.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.760
"We're all that's left?"

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:52.240
"Hey, hey!

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.200
It's okay, alright?

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.240
We're gonna figure it out.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.280
We have each other."

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.340
What also likely contributed to Marvel's Runaways
getting axed is the fact that Jeph Loeb is

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.660
reportedly lined up to leave Marvel Television.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.670
The Hollywood Reporter claims that Loeb, who
has served as the executive VP of Marvel TV

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:14.740
since 2010, will make a formal announcement
of his departure before the end of the month,

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.360
at some point ahead of Thanksgiving Day on
Thursday, November 28.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.960
With Runaways on its way out, there are only
a handful of Loeb-produced Marvel series in

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.300
existence: Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D., which will
end with its upcoming seventh season; Hulu's

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.980
horror-themed Marvel show Helstrom;
and four adult-oriented animated series.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.980
It seems evident that Marvel Studios is taking
over the television side of things from, well,

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.160
the actual television division of the company.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:44.000
Marvel's Runaways isn't the only show to have
become a casualty amid all this change, and

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.660
it sadly may not be the last.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:50.260
Here's hoping that what Marvel Studios has
planned with its Disney+ series will be worth

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:54.810
all the heartache of watching so many shows
get snapped away, and that the few remaining

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:58.560
Marvel TV-backed projects live to see at least
a few seasons.

